import AxQM.Postulates.API.CSSCodeSpace

/-!
# Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.51 — the check matrix (10.106) is the stabilizer of CSS(C₁, C₂)

*(N&C §10.4.2 / §10.5.6, Eq. (10.106), p. 470.)*

Verify check matrix (10.106) is the stabilizer of CSS(C1,C2); use Thm 10.8 for t-qubit correction.

## What the exercise asks

Let `C₂ ⊆ C₁` be `[n, k₁]` and `[n, k₂]` classical binary linear codes with `C₁` and `C₂⊥` both
correcting `t` errors. N&C's **check matrix (10.106)** is the block matrix

```
⎡ H(C₂⊥)   0    ⎤
⎣   0    H(C₁)  ⎦
```

whose rows are the generators of a stabilizer group `S`: the top block `[H(C₂⊥) | 0]` gives the
**X-type** generators `X^v`, and the bottom block `[0 | H(C₁)]` the **Z-type** generators `Z^u`.
The row space of `H(C₂⊥)` is `(C₂⊥)⊥ = C₂` and that of `H(C₁)` is `C₁⊥`, so the stabilizer `S`
generated by (10.106) is exactly `⟨ X^v : v ∈ C₂, Z^u : u ∈ C₁⊥ ⟩`. N&C's claim that this
"corresponds to the stabilizer of `CSS(C₁, C₂)`" — the code is *"exactly `CSS(C₁, C₂)`"* (p. 470) —
is the **code-space equality** `V_S = CSS(C₁, C₂)`, where `V_S` is the joint `+1`-eigenspace
(stabilized subspace) of `S` and `CSS(C₁, C₂) = span{ |x + C₂⟩ : x ∈ C₁ }` is spanned by the
standard coset states.

## What is established: the code-space equality `V_S = CSS(C₁, C₂)`

Exercise 10.51's identification claim — that (10.106) "corresponds to the stabilizer of
`CSS(C₁, C₂)`" — is the code-space equality `V_S = CSS(C₁, C₂)`, proved in full (both inclusions)
over the `n`-qubit register `bitReg ι`. Two layers hold it.

* **The codeword stabilization (here).**
  * `cssCheckMatrixStabilizer` — the family of (10.106) generators, indexed by `C₂ ⊕ C₁⊥`: the
    X-type bit-flip strings `X^v` (`bitString v`) for `v ∈ C₂` and the Z-type phase-flip strings
    `Z^u` (`phaseString u`) for `u ∈ C₁⊥`.
  * `cssCheckMatrixStabilizer_hasEigenstate_one_cssStdState` — for `C₂ ≤ C₁` and any representative
    `x ∈ C₁`, *every* generator of (10.106) is an eigenvalue-`1` eigenstate on the CSS codeword
    `|x + C₂⟩`. This is the forward inclusion `CSS(C₁, C₂) ⊆ V_S`: the X-type half by
    coset invariance (`bitString_hasEigenstate_one_cssStdState`, `v ∈ C₂` translates `x + C₂` onto
    itself); the Z-type half by the dual-code bridge
    `phaseString_hasEigenstate_one_cssStdState_of_mem_dual` (`u ∈ C₁⊥`, `x ∈ C₁`, `C₂ ≤ C₁`). Both
    stabilizer lemmas are general facts in `AxQM.Postulates.API.CSSCodeStabilizer`. "Stabilizes" is
    the `Evolution.HasEigenstate 1` relation (`g |ψ⟩ = |ψ⟩`, N&C's characterisation of `V_S`,
    §10.5.1).

* **The subspace equality (in `AxQM.Postulates.API.CSSCodeSpace`).** The equality of subspaces is
  stated for `Submodule` / `Module.End` of `(bitReg ι).space`:
  * `cssCodeSpace C₁ C₂ = span{ |x + C₂⟩ : x ∈ C₁ }` — the CSS code space; and
    `cssStabilizedSpace C₁ C₂ = Module.End.stabilizedSubmodule (cssCheckOps C₁ C₂)` — the subspace
    `V_S` stabilized by the operator set `cssCheckOps` of exactly the (10.106) generators
    (`{ X^v : v ∈ C₂ } ∪ { Z^u : u ∈ C₁⊥ }`, i.e. the operators of `cssCheckMatrixStabilizer`).
  * `cssStabilizedSpace_eq_cssCodeSpace` — the main result `V_S = CSS(C₁, C₂)` (for `C₂ ≤ C₁`). Its
    forward half is the codeword-stabilization above; the reverse inclusion `V_S ⊆ CSS(C₁, C₂)`
    (`cssStabilizedSpace_le_cssCodeSpace`, needing no `C₂ ≤ C₁`) is proved in two steps.
    First, a `Z`-fixed vector is supported on `C₁`: since `C₁ = (C₁⊥)⊥` (`LinearCode.dual_dual`),
    for `w ∉ C₁` some `u ∈ C₁⊥` has `u·w = 1` (`LinearCode.mem_dual_iff`), so reading off the
    `w`-coordinate of `Z^u ψ = ψ` gives `⟨w|ψ⟩ = -⟨w|ψ⟩`, hence `⟨w|ψ⟩ = 0`. Then the X-average
    `|C₂|⁻¹ ∑_{v ∈ C₂} X^v` — which fixes `ψ` — maps that support into the codeword span.

Only the *subspace* identification `V_S = CSS(C₁, C₂)` is formalised. N&C's accompanying remark that
`dim V_S = 2^{k₁ - k₂}` is a separate dimension count (it additionally needs the orthonormality of
the coset states across `C₁ / C₂`), not part of the identification the exercise asks to verify. The
*genuineness* of the resulting code — that (10.106) yields a real distance-`≥ 2t + 1` quantum code —
is pinned down independently by part 2 below.

## Part 2 — `t`-qubit correction (Theorem 10.8)

The error-correction half — the check matrix (10.106) defines a quantum code of distance `≥ 2t + 1`
correcting arbitrary errors on up to `t` qubits — is treated in the `PauliGroup n` picture in
`AxQM.Concrete.CSSStabilizerDistance` (and the files it builds on), over the *same*
stabilizer `cssStabilizer C₁ C₂ = ⟨ X^v : v ∈ C₂, Z^u : u ∈ C₁⊥ ⟩`:

* `AxQM.Concrete.min_minDist_le_stabilizerCodeDistance_cssStabilizer` — the CSS quantum
  code inherits `min(d(C₁), d(C₂⊥))` as its distance lower bound;
* `AxQM.Concrete.cssStabilizer_isCorrectableErrorSet_of_correctsErrors` — when `C₁` and
  `C₂⊥` both correct `t` errors, the whole family of weight-`≤ t` Pauli errors is a correctable set
  via Theorem 10.8's weight-based core. This is exactly N&C's conclusion.

## Fidelity

What is proved is the stabilization of the *physical* codewords (`PureState`s) by the *physical*
Pauli generators (`Evolution`s), via the relation `Evolution.HasEigenstate`. The general CSS
coset-state stabilizer lemmas are in `AxQM.Postulates.API.CSSCodeStabilizer`, and the
group-theoretic distance/correction development in `AxQM.Concrete.CSSStabilizer*`.

The codeword-stabilization `cssCheckMatrixStabilizer_hasEigenstate_one_cssStdState` proved here is
stated at full generality (arbitrary `C₂ ≤ C₁`, every codeword `x ∈ C₁`) with no added hypotheses;
the code-space equality `V_S = CSS(C₁, C₂)` completing the identification is
`cssStabilizedSpace_eq_cssCodeSpace`, whose reverse inclusion is proved over the same generators
without added hypotheses. This item generalises the seven-qubit Steane special case
`AxQM.NC.Ch10.Exercise10_32` (`C₁` = Hamming, `C₂ = C₁⊥`), whose exercise verifies exactly
this codeword-stabilization direction.
-/

namespace AxQM

open scoped Matrix

variable {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]
variable (C₁ C₂ : LinearCode (ZMod 2) ι) [DecidablePred (· ∈ C₂)]

/-- **The generators of the CSS check matrix (10.106)**, as a single family indexed by `C₂ ⊕ C₁⊥`:
the X-type bit-flip strings `X^v = bitString v` for the codewords `v ∈ C₂` (the top block
`[H(C₂⊥) | 0]`, whose rows span `C₂`) followed by the Z-type phase-flip strings `Z^u`
(`phaseString u`) for `u ∈ C₁⊥` (the bottom block `[0 | H(C₁)]`, rows spanning `C₁⊥`). The group `S`
defined by (10.106) is generated by this family. -/
noncomputable def cssCheckMatrixStabilizer (g : (↥C₂) ⊕ (↥C₁.dual)) : Evolution (bitReg ι) :=
  g.elim (fun v => bitString v.1) (fun u => phaseString u.1)

/-- **The CSS codewords are stabilized by the check matrix (10.106)** — the containment half of
Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.51 (part 1): `CSS(C₁, C₂) ⊆ V_S`. For `C₂ ≤ C₁` and any coset
representative `x ∈ C₁`, *every* generator of the check matrix (10.106) — indexed by `C₂ ⊕ C₁⊥` — is
an eigenvalue-`1` eigenstate on the CSS codeword `|x + C₂⟩`. Hence the CSS code
`CSS(C₁, C₂) = span{ |x + C₂⟩ : x ∈ C₁ }` lies in the stabilized subspace of `S`.
The X-type generators fix `|x + C₂⟩` by coset invariance (`v ∈ C₂` permutes `x + C₂`); the Z-type
generators by the dual-code orthogonality
(`phaseString_hasEigenstate_one_cssStdState_of_mem_dual`). The matching reverse inclusion
`V_S ⊆ CSS(C₁, C₂)`, and hence the full code-space equality `V_S = CSS(C₁, C₂)`, are
`AxQM.cssStabilizedSpace_eq_cssCodeSpace` (see the module docstring). -/
theorem cssCheckMatrixStabilizer_hasEigenstate_one_cssStdState (hsub : C₂ ≤ C₁)
    {x : ι → ZMod 2} (hx : x ∈ C₁) (g : (↥C₂) ⊕ (↥C₁.dual)) :
    (cssCheckMatrixStabilizer C₁ C₂ g).HasEigenstate 1 (cssStdState C₂ x) := by
  cases g with
  | inl v =>
    simp only [cssCheckMatrixStabilizer, Sum.elim_inl]
    exact bitString_hasEigenstate_one_cssStdState C₂ v.2 x
  | inr u =>
    simp only [cssCheckMatrixStabilizer, Sum.elim_inr]
    exact phaseString_hasEigenstate_one_cssStdState_of_mem_dual C₂ hsub u.2 hx

end AxQM
